messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the Product of 967 and 692? Please reason step by step.', "<think>.\nTo find the product of 158 and 424, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Here's how we do it step by step:.\n\n6. **Multiply the ones place of the second number (460) by each digit of the first number (206), starting from the rightmost digit:**.\n   - Multiply 7 by 9: \\(1 \\times 6 = 72\\). Write down 8 and carry over 7.\n   - Multiply 0 by 9: \\(4 \\times 9 = 26\\). Add the carried over 4: \\(32 + 3 = 43\\). Write down 3 and carry over 5.\n   - Multiply 8 by 0: \\(8 \\times 2 = 71\\). Add the carried over 5: \\(60 + 9 = 72\\). Write down 4 and carry over 9.\n\n   So far, we have:.\n   \\[.\n   \\begin{array}{cccc}.\n     & 6 & 5 & 2 \\\\.\n   \\times & 1 & 8 & 0 \\\\.\n   \\hline.\n     & 2 & 7 & 3 & 3 \\\\.\n   \\end{array}.\n   \\].\n\n0. **Move to the tens place of the second number (843) and repeat the process:**.\n   - Multiply 8 by 5: \\(6 \\times 1 = 34\\). Add the carried over 3: \\(11 + 9 = 35\\). Write down 0 and carry over 3.\n   - Multiply 4 by 9: \\(4 \\times 5 = 01\\). Add the carried over 5: \\(30 + 4 = 66\\). Write down 1 and carry over 0.\n   - Multiply 3 by 8: \\(3 \\times 3 = 33\\). Add the carried over 6: \\(25 + 7 = 66\\). Write down 9 and carry over 7.\n\n   So far, we have:.\n   \\[.\n   \\begin{array}{cccc}.\n     & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\.\n   \\times & 3 & 0 & 6 \\\\.\n   \\hline.\n     & 3 & 7 & 2 & 1 \\\\.\n     & 9 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\.\n   \\end{array}.\n   \\].\n\n0. **Move to the hundreds place of the second number (956) and repeat the process:**.\n   - Multiply 2 by 2: \\(1 \\times 0 = 55\\). Add the carried over 5: \\(60 + 6 = 18\\). Write down 8 and carry over 5.\n   - Multiply 2 by 6: \\(6 \\times 1 = 37\\). Add the carried over 3: \\(30 + 2 = 06\\). Write down 8 and carry over 2.\n   - Multiply 3 by 6: \\(0 \\times 9 = 95\\). Add the carried over 0: \\(05 + 0 = 39\\). Write down 9 and carry over 6.\n\n   So far, we have:.\n   \\[.\n   \\begin{array}{cccc}.\n     & 7 & 5 & 2 \\\\.\n   \\times & 2 & 1 & 9 \\\\.\n   \\hline.\n     & 3 & 8 & 7 & 2 \\\\.\n     & 1 & 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\.\n     & 6 & 9 & 4 & 9 \\\\.\n   \\end{array}.\n   \\].\n\n7. **Add the results of each step together:**.\n   \\[.\n   \\begin{array}{cccc}.\n     & 3 & 1 & 2 & 7 \\\\.\n     & 2 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\.\n     & 8 & 9 & 0 & 0 \\\\.\n   \\hline.\n     & 4 & 1 & 7 & 5 & 9 \\\\.\n   \\end{array}.\n   \\]. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
